To precisely calculate the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ branching ratio, we need to accurately determine the shape of the differential decay rate, particularly the contribution from the operator corresponding to the leptonic contact interaction, which affects the rate at large hadron recoil angles. Despite the lack of a direct contribution to this specific operator, its impact on the shape of the differential decay rate can be assessed through relationships with comparable processes, specifically $Bightarrow K_{S/B}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ and $B_s^0ightarrow \gamma\gamma$. Given that the $C_9$ component is already well-constrained by the $Bightarrow K_{S/B}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ results, we can infer that any significant deviation from the standard model's prediction for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rate would indicate new physics affecting the shape of the rate at large hadron recoil angles. To this end, we have performed a fit to available data from the LHCb experiment, incorporating both the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ and $Bightarrow K_{S/B}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ results to assess the consistency of the shape of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rate with the standard model. Given the limited statistics of the current data, we find that we cannot rule out new physics affecting the shape of the rate at large hadron recoil angles, although it would need to be consistent with the constraints derived from the $Bightarrow K_{S/B}\mu^{+}\mu^{-}$ results to do so. To further explore this possibility, we suggest increasing the recoil angle bin size in future measurements. Additionally, we recommend that experiments consider measuring the polarisation of the $D_s^{*−}$ hadron to gain a deeper understanding of the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ differential decay rate. Furthermore, we encourage theoretical investigations into possible new physics scenarios that could influence the shape of the rate at large hadron recoil angles. To this end, we have provided a set of effective lagrangian terms corresponding to the leptonic contact interaction that could be used as a starting point for such investigations
